<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rekhmire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rekhmire was an ancient Egyptian noble and official of the 18th Dynasty who served as Governor of the Town, the mayor of Thebes, and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> under Thutmosis III and Amenhotep II, around 1400 BCE. The nephew of the vizier User, he was also High Priest of Heliopolis. He built a richly decorated tomb, TT100, at Sheikh Abd el-Qurna in the Theban Necropolis, whose lively and well-preserved scenes of daily life illustrate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>; it also holds a full copy of the text on the duties of the vizier known as the Installation of the Vizier. The cause of his downfall is unclear: he is suspected to have fallen into disgrace and been deposed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rekhmire.jpg" alt="Rekhmire" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heidi Kontkanen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rekhmire_sits_on_a_stool_in_his_TT100_tomb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
